title the round that sat in the stash, and the scratch that reddened the fleet
prompt the standing GRASS lap -- audit, reds first, one keystone
rota lap 4661, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; hand-advanced past rows 0-3, all read today. Its cardinal seat, foundations/20260905-154954_the-clock-and-the-mark.md, is the very law the recovered round's guard enforces -- a number announced for planned work is a forecast.
think Opened on the concrete fact rather than the plan: HEAD equalled xy/main, the tree was clean, and git stash list held 17 entries. stash@{0} was written by fleet_round_open.sh ten seconds before my open and carried a whole round -- paper, ledger row, two repaired tool files, a session log -- none of it committed. The previous lap was cut mid-send.
think Measured the box rather than guessing at it. For each of the 17 stashes, every path it touches against HEAD: only stash@{0} holds paths no branch carries (4 of them), plus one older stash holding a single fold shelf. The dead-letter box is otherwise harmless, so the recovery is one round rather than a backlog.
think The roster's own guard had already said so. The cold pass read stash_record red -- unlanded=1 -- which is the guard REDS %464 built for exactly this: a session log parked at a round open that nothing else carries.
think Verified the recovered repair before trusting it: tools/fixtures/a/announced_length_control.sh reads pass=25 fail=0, and the witness is GREEN. The repair gives the scan a mktemp refusal at exit 2 and a #listing-complete sentinel written last and required back.
think Re-measured its census before booking the row, since an unshared row may still be corrected. The row claimed a singleton over seven scans, five of which use mktemp. Calling mktemp is not refusing on mktemp. The class is eleven tracked scans that redirect a block into a scratch and read it back; five carry an explicit || exit, five carry set -e alone under which a failed assignment ends the run, and one carried set -u and neither.
think Proven in this shell rather than read off the source: set -eu exits 1 on a failed mktemp, set -u alone continues with an empty name. That one option is the whole difference between the broken scan and its ten siblings.
think The class was counted three times and grew twice, each time because a counter read one spelling: seven counted mktemp calls, ten followed the literal redirect target, eleven followed the alias plant_liveness_scan.sh assigns one line after its redirect.
obs Then I caused a red and the red was the finding. I verified the recovered repair in a git worktree at .lap/verify -- gitignored, and the baton's own room for a lap's scratch -- while this ship's cold roster pass was still running. That pass read harness_roster unresolved=1 -> 2, one past a no-slack ceiling, and the second unresolved named .lap/verify/tools/fixtures/c/caravan_reply_control.sh: the same file as the first, counted twice because it stood on disk twice.
obs A guard that walks with find reads whatever stands under the root, including .lap/, session-output/, loops/ and a worktree parked under any of them. Planted deliberately afterward: copying mantra/ into .lap/probe/ took copy_sameness from verdict=ok to verdict=drift. Its live paths reading had stood at 45 against a true 23 for as long as my worktree existed.
obs Both repaired to ask git rather than spell a path list. The elder lines excluded vendor, gratitude and seed by name, which is a claim about what somebody remembered; the ignore rules are the tree's own statement of what it disowns, kept current by the hand that opens a room. git check-ignore is batched through --stdin for the 3,800-candidate corpus and called per path for the 23-path one, and each scan prints ignored_filtered so a pen with no git reads as unfiltered rather than silently unread.
obs Third firing of one shape in a single round: this round's own write-up spelled the pen's forecast literally, and the repaired guard read the paper, the card and the ledger row as three fresh forecasts by three living pages. Fixed in the prose rather than in the meter -- a page that describes a forecast can make none. The scan's header already names the same shape twice, for a table and for its own quoted output.
obs Both rows are born on shelves, and both renumbered on the rebase -- a peer published %720 at 20260911.205727, a row about this same dead-letter box, while this lap was recovering from it. A published number never moves and an unshared row derives above every number the spine has bound, so mine read %721 and %722. reds_pin_capacity reads rows_that_fit=0, every other pin row is OPEN, and the only foldable row is this round's own; keeping the dead lap's fold of %717 and shelving both new rows leaves the fleet's pin at 38,967 against a 40,960 bound, which is real headroom on a pin that has been deadlock-adjacent all day.
